```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router0
diagnosis_text:
The fault is on router0, not on mswitch1. The mswitch1 trunk is functioning
correctly: br_vlan10 (eth0.10 ↔ eth4.10) and br_vlan20 (eth0.20 ↔ eth4.20)
properly bridge switch1 to router0's VLAN sub-interfaces, which is confirmed
by Location A hosts successfully reaching their gateways (172.16.10.1 and
172.16.20.1). The ICMP reply PC4 receives is "Destination Net Unreachable"
sourced from 172.16.10.1 — router0's own VLAN 10 interface — meaning the
packet arrives at the gateway but router0 has no onward route.

The root cause is that /etc/frr/ripd.conf on router0 has been emptied
(the file exists but is 0 bytes). The /etc/frr/daemons file has ripd=yes,
so FRR attempts to start the RIP daemon but loads an empty configuration,
giving ripd no network statements and no interfaces to operate on. As a
result the frr.service is in a failed state and router0 has zero RIP-learned
routes. Its routing table contains only directly connected subnets
(172.16.10/20, 172.20.1, 192.168.1, 10.16.1) plus two static kernel entries
for 172.18.50-51. It has no route to Location C or any other remote site, so
every non-local packet is dropped with an ICMP Net Unreachable.

Corrective fix: restore /etc/frr/ripd.conf on router0 with the correct RIP
configuration (at minimum: "router rip / network 10.0.0.0/8 / version 2",
mirroring router1's working config), then restart the FRR service
("systemctl restart frr"). Once ripd re-establishes adjacency with router1
over the 10.16.1.0/24 WAN link, router0 will re-learn all remote prefixes
and Location A hosts will regain full connectivity.
=== END ===
```